#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""session-continuity -- bring an orchestrator's session back when mail waits for it.

THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES. An orchestrator is a registration on the bus and a
workpaper on disk. Its process is not part of it. A background session ends, the
desktop app moves a conversation, and the handle reads gone while the work it
names is still open. Mail then sits in that inbox until somebody looks. The wake
rail beside this program tells a live session it has mail and refuses a gone one,
by its own law. This program is the other half: it starts the recorded session
again, under its owner, from its own transcript, and ends it when it goes quiet.

WHAT IT DELIVERS. One fixed sentence, BOOT_SENTENCE below, carrying no
interpolation at all. It says the rail resumed you and that your inbox is what to
read. Everything the session then does comes from the bus and from its own
workpaper. `--law` re-derives the field set from the string at runtime and the
program refuses to run when it carries a field, so the claim is a command anybody
can run rather than a grep of the author's honesty.

A RESUME IS NOT A FORK, AND ONE RESUME FORM MAKES IT ONE. `--resume` continues
one session id and one transcript. A session the harness has already run in the
background carries its own saved options, and flags passed to a resume of that
session start a COPY under a new session id; it says so on stderr, after the copy
exists. Measured 2026-09-25: a first resume with flags continued session
0be4ddbb, the next resume of it with the same flags started 3273f5c5 carrying a
copy of its whole history, and a resume with no flags continued 3273f5c5 and kept
its id.

So there are two forms. A session the harness lists with kind `background` is
resumed bare and wakes with the name, model and permission flag it already has. A
session with no row takes the flags, which set those three the first time. A
listing that cannot be read is refused, because a flagged resume of the wrong
case is the one outcome nothing later can undo.

THE TWIN CHECK, BOTH HALVES, BEFORE ANY RESUME.

  1. No live process on this machine carries the handle's session id. Read from
     the harness's own session records through `session-guard`, which is the one
     reading of that question the wake rail and the sweeper also take.
  2. No live session carries the handle's recorded address or its handle name.
     The app can continue a conversation under a new session id while the bus
     still records the old one, and the name is what survives that move.

Either half true means the session is already here. The handle is handed to the
wake rail's adapter for a nudge and nothing is resumed.

THE RAIL ENDS WHAT IT STARTED. A resumed session does not exit by itself.
Measured 2026-09-25: one sat idle 29 minutes holding 351 MB and was still there.
So a pass stops a session this rail resumed once the harness listing reads it
idle and its transcript has not grown for the threshold. Two readings, because
the listing alone would end a session between two turns of work. `claude stop`
keeps the conversation, so the next pass resumes the same session from the same
transcript. Only what this rail resumed, named in its state file, and only once.

WHAT IT REFUSES, RATHER THAN WARNS ABOUT. A handle whose record carries no
session id: there is nothing to resume, and searching for one is three chances to
resume a stranger. A recorded working directory this account cannot enter: a
session started somewhere else is a session in the wrong place. A bus directory
other than the one /etc/orchestrate.conf names: this program starts processes,
and a bus nobody declared is not the board it may act on.

THE HOLD IS THE SIGN-OUT CASE. A resume that exits non-zero, or whose process is
not alive thirty seconds later, writes a hold carrying the exit code and the last
line of stderr, and sends one line to the `human` handle. No handle of this
account is resumed again until `--clear-hold` clears it. A harness signed out
refuses every resume, and a rail that kept trying would start a login attempt
every two minutes and say nothing anybody reads. The hold is a file, so the
sign-out alarm can read the same answer later.

Nudging and stopping go on under a hold. Neither is the act that was failing.

AN ACTING PASS PRUNES ITS OWN STATE DIRECTORY. A handle's file that nothing has
written to in thirty days is removed, unless it carries a hold or names a session
this rail resumed and has not stopped. Both of those say something is outstanding,
and outstanding does not expire.

  session-continuity --check           print what a pass would do; do nothing
  session-continuity --once            one pass; this is what the timer runs
  session-continuity --once <handle>   the same pass, acting on those handles
                                       alone. What the board says is unchanged:
                                       every other handle is still listed with
                                       the action it would have taken. It is
                                       what brings one orchestrator back without
                                       waking every one an account holds
  session-continuity --law             print the boot sentence and prove it
                                       carries no interpolation
  session-continuity --clear-hold <h>  clear that handle's hold

  --idle-stop-minutes <n>              how long a session this rail resumed may
                                       sit quiet before a pass ends it. It is
                                       there to drive a proof; the units pass
                                       none and take the default

EXIT CODES. 0 the pass ran. 1 the sentence law is broken. 2 usage. 3 a wall: the
bus is not the declared one, session-guard is missing, the wake rail this program
hands a twin to is missing, or the harness command line is absent.
"""

import datetime
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import pwd
import re
import shutil
import string
import subprocess
import sys
import time

# ------------------------------------------------------------- THE SENTENCE
#
# NO format field, and no other way to build delivered text. The wake rail
# beside this program allows itself one field, the bus directory, because its
# sentence names the bus to read. This one names nothing: the session it reaches
# finds its own bus the way it found it before.
BOOT_SENTENCE = (
    "The continuity rail resumed you because mail waits for you. "
    "Run the orchestrate skill's resume operation, then read your inbox."
)

# %-style and str.format are the two ways a Python string interpolates. The law
# check below proves the sentence carries neither.
PERCENT_FIELD = re.compile(r"%[^%]")

# What a model id looks like, which is anything the harness accepts after
# --model. The point of the test is what it excludes: the harness's own
# `<synthetic>` placeholder, and an empty field.
MODEL_ID = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$")

# The bracketed reference the agent listing appends to a name it shows twice. A
# handle records the listing's whole string as its address, and the harness's
# session record carries the name without it, so a comparison of the two has to
# take the reference off first.
LISTING_REF = re.compile(r"\s*\[[0-9a-f]{4,}\]$")

CONF = os.environ.get("ORCHESTRATE_CONF", "/etc/orchestrate.conf")
BIN_DIR = "/opt/commonclaw/bin"
HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
ROLE = "orchestrator"
# How long the resumed process gets to appear in the harness's session records.
# A background session writes its record as it starts; thirty seconds is the
# measured start plus room, and it is the whole cost of a pass that resumed.
ALIVE_AFTER_SECS = 30
# A `claude --bg` call returns as soon as the session is started. A call still
# running after this long has not returned at all, which is its own failure.
SPAWN_TIMEOUT_SECS = 120
# How long a session this rail resumed may sit quiet before the rail ends it.
# `--idle-stop-minutes` moves it for a proof; the units pass none.
IDLE_STOP_MINUTES = 15


def law_fields():
    return {f for _, f, _, _ in string.Formatter().parse(BOOT_SENTENCE) if f is not None}


def lawful():
    return not law_fields() and not PERCENT_FIELD.search(BOOT_SENTENCE)


def log(msg):
    sys.stderr.write(msg + "\n")
    sys.stderr.flush()


def now_iso():
    return datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).replace(microsecond=0).isoformat()


# --------------------------------------------------------------- the settings
#
# The bus and the fallback model come from the orchestrate settings seam's
# machine layer, which is the file every tool here already reads. This program
# adds no conf of its own: one more file would be one more place the bus is
# named, and two names for one bus is the fault this rail is most able to cause.
def conf_value(key):
    pattern = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*%s[ \t]*[:=][ \t]*(.*)$" % re.escape(key))
    try:
        with open(CONF, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                m = pattern.match(line)
                if not m:
                    continue
                raw = m.group(1).split("#", 1)[0].strip()
                if len(raw) >= 2 and raw[0] == raw[-1] and raw[0] in "\"'":
                    raw = raw[1:-1]
                return raw or None
    except OSError:
        return None
    return None


def beside(name):
    """A sibling of this program, or the copy the claw installs.

    The four rails land in one directory on a claw, so a sibling is the ordinary
    answer. A program read out of a source tree has no siblings, and there the
    installed copy is the one that agrees with the running rail.
    """
    local = os.path.join(HERE, name)
    if os.path.exists(local):
        return local
    return os.path.join(BIN_DIR, name)


def state_dir():
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME") or os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state")
    return os.path.join(base, "commonclaw", "continuity")


def harness():
    """The agent command line, or "" when this account has none.

    It is installed per account, under a home directory, so a unit's minimal
    PATH does not find it and the home path is the fallback.
    """
    found = shutil.which("claude")
    if found:
        return found
    guess = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/bin/claude")
    return guess if os.access(guess, os.X_OK) else ""


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the readings
def load_module(path, name):
    """A program beside this one, loaded as a module, or (None, why).

    Without bytecode: this file runs from a skill tree and from the machine's bin
    directory, and a cache written into either is a file nobody shipped.
    """
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        return None, "no %s at %s" % (name, path)
    sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
    try:
        loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(name.replace("-", "_"), path)
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)
        mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
        loader.exec_module(mod)
    except Exception as exc:  # a broken sibling is reported, never half-used
        return None, "%s at %s did not load: %s: %s" % (name, path, type(exc).__name__, exc)
    return mod, ""


def unread_count(bus_dir, handle):
    """How many messages sit past the handle's cursor, and the newest one's id."""
    inbox = os.path.join(bus_dir, "inbox", "%s.jsonl" % handle)
    try:
        with open(inbox, "rb") as fh:
            lines = [b for b in fh.read().splitlines() if b.strip()]
    except OSError:
        return 0, None
    cursor = 0
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(bus_dir, "cursors", "%s.cursor" % handle), "r") as fh:
            cursor = int(fh.read().strip() or 0)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        cursor = 0
    if len(lines) <= cursor:
        return 0, None
    newest = None
    try:
        newest = json.loads(lines[-1].decode("utf-8", "replace")).get("id")
    except ValueError:
        newest = None
    return len(lines) - cursor, newest


def saved_sessions(listing):
    """The sessions that carry their own saved options: the background rows.

    None when the listing could not be read, which is not the same answer as an
    empty one. One says nothing is saved, the other says nothing is known.
    """
    if listing is None:
        return None
    return {sid for sid, row in listing.items() if row.get("kind") == "background"}


def listed_sessions(cmd):
    """Every session the harness lists, by session id, or None when it cannot be read.

    One subprocess a pass, and two questions come out of it: which sessions carry
    saved options, and which of the ones this rail resumed are idle now.
    """
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run([cmd, "agents", "--json", "--all"], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                              stderr=subprocess.PIPE, timeout=60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    try:
        data = json.loads(proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace") or "null")
    except ValueError:
        return None
    rows = data if isinstance(data, list) else (data or {}).get("agents")
    if not isinstance(rows, list):
        return None
    return {r["sessionId"]: r for r in rows
            if isinstance(r, dict) and isinstance(r.get("sessionId"), str) and r["sessionId"]}


def transcript_path(session):
    """The transcript file for one session id, or "".

    The project directory is named for the directory a session started in, which
    is not always the one the handle recorded, so the file is found by its own
    name. The session id is unique, so the search cannot land on a stranger.
    """
    root = os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/projects")
    try:
        for project in sorted(os.listdir(root)):
            candidate = os.path.join(root, project, "%s.jsonl" % session)
            if os.path.isfile(candidate):
                return candidate
    except OSError:
        return ""
    return ""


def transcript_model(session):
    """The model the transcript's last assistant turn records, or "".

    A resumed session that came back on another model is a charter running
    against dispositions it was not written for. The transcript is the only
    record of which one it was, and its path is not derivable from the handle:
    the project directory is named for the directory the session started in,
    which is not always the one the handle recorded. So the file is found by its
    own name, which is the session id and is unique.

    A TURN THE HARNESS WROTE FOR ITSELF NAMES NO MODEL. It records the word
    `<synthetic>` in the model field, and a resume asking for that exits with a
    usage error. Read on this machine 2026-09-25: four of six resumable
    orchestrator transcripts ended on such a turn. So a value not shaped like a
    model id is passed over, and the newest one that is shaped like one answers.
    """
    path = transcript_path(session)
    if not path:
        return "", ""
    model = ""
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line:
                    continue
                try:
                    rec = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                msg = rec.get("message") if isinstance(rec, dict) else None
                if not (isinstance(msg, dict) and msg.get("role") == "assistant"):
                    continue
                if MODEL_ID.match(str(msg.get("model") or "")):
                    model = str(msg["model"])
    except OSError:
        return "", path
    return model, path


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- state
def state_path(handle):
    return os.path.join(state_dir(), "%s.json" % re.sub(r"[^A-Za-z0-9_.-]", "_", handle))


def read_state(handle):
    try:
        with open(state_path(handle), "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            data = json.load(fh)
        return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}


def write_state(handle, patch):
    data = read_state(handle)
    data.update(patch)
    data["handle"] = handle
    path = state_path(handle)
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        tmp = path + ".tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            json.dump(data, fh, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except OSError as exc:
        log("could not write %s: %s" % (path, exc))
    return data


def holds():
    """Every hold this account carries, as (handle, hold) pairs.

    A hold is per handle and it stops every handle, because what a hold usually
    means is that the harness itself refuses. Trying the next handle would find
    the same refusal and write the same line.
    """
    out = []
    try:
        names = sorted(os.listdir(state_dir()))
    except OSError:
        return out
    for name in names:
        if not name.endswith(".json"):
            continue
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(state_dir(), name), "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                data = json.load(fh)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        if isinstance(data, dict) and isinstance(data.get("hold"), dict):
            out.append((data.get("handle") or name[:-5], data["hold"]))
    return out


def prune_state(older_than=30 * 86400):
    """Drop state for a handle nothing has written to in a month.

    A handle that is gone leaves its file behind forever otherwise, the way the
    wake rail's dedupe state did before it pruned. Every wake, resume and stop
    rewrites the file, so the modification time is the handle's own activity and
    thirty days is far outside any window a pass reads.

    TWO FILES SURVIVE WHATEVER THEIR AGE. A file carrying a hold is the one
    record that this account is stopped, and it is cleared by a person running
    --clear-hold, which may be a long time after the hold was written. A file
    naming a resumed_session is a session this rail started and has not stopped,
    and removing it would leave that session running with nothing left that
    knows to end it. Age is the wrong reading for both: each says something is
    outstanding, and outstanding does not expire.

    Returns the handles removed, so a pass can say what it dropped.
    """
    cutoff = time.time() - older_than
    dropped = []
    try:
        names = sorted(os.listdir(state_dir()))
    except OSError:
        return dropped
    for name in names:
        if not name.endswith(".json"):
            continue
        path = os.path.join(state_dir(), name)
        try:
            if os.path.getmtime(path) >= cutoff:
                continue
        except OSError:
            continue
        try:
            with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                data = json.load(fh)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            data = {}
        if not isinstance(data, dict):
            data = {}
        if isinstance(data.get("hold"), dict) or data.get("resumed_session"):
            continue
        try:
            os.remove(path)
        except OSError:
            continue
        dropped.append(data.get("handle") or name[:-5])
    return dropped


# ------------------------------------------------------------------- the acts
def tell_human(bus_dir, handle, reason):
    """One line to the `human` handle, and nothing else anywhere on the bus.

    The wake rail writes to no bus at all, because a rail that filed its own
    failures into an inbox would raise unread mail and nudge itself forever. This
    program writes to one handle that the rail skips by name and that nobody
    drains. So the line reaches the person who has to sign in, and it starts no
    loop.

    The body carries the handle and the reason. It never carries the harness's
    own output, which is where a token or a path a person did not choose to share
    would appear.
    """
    bus = beside("bus")
    if not os.access(bus, os.X_OK):
        log("no bus command at %s, so the hold on %s reached nobody" % (bus, handle))
        return False
    env = dict(os.environ, SESSION_BUS_DIR=bus_dir)
    body = "%s could not be resumed: %s. No handle of this account is resumed until the hold is cleared." % (
        handle, reason)
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [bus, "send", "session-continuity", "human",
             "continuity rail: %s is held" % handle, body],
            env=env, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, timeout=30)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        log("could not tell human about %s: %s: %s" % (handle, type(exc).__name__, exc))
        return False
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        log("bus send about %s exited %d" % (handle, proc.returncode))
        return False
    return True


def nudge(rail, adapter_dir, bus_dir, handle, rec, pid):
    """Hand one handle to the wake rail's adapter, with the rail's own sentence.

    The sentence is taken from the rail rather than written here. Two copies of
    one law drift, and the rail's `--law` would then prove nothing about what a
    session actually received.

    The session id is left out of the payload on purpose. A twin found by name is
    the case where the app moved the conversation to a new id, so the adapter's
    equality test against the bus record would refuse the very session that is
    live. What identifies the target here is its own pid and its own socket.
    """
    adapter = os.path.join(adapter_dir, "claude")
    if not os.access(adapter, os.X_OK):
        return {"outcome": "failed", "reason": "no adapter at %s" % adapter}
    payload = {
        "handle": handle, "bus_dir": bus_dir, "session": "", "pid": pid,
        "owner": rec.get("owner") or "", "address": rec.get("address") or "",
        "pane": rec.get("pane") or "", "cwd": rec.get("cwd") or "",
        "unread": 0, "text": rail.nudge_text(bus_dir),
    }
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [adapter, "deliver"], input=json.dumps(payload).encode("utf-8"),
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, timeout=30)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        return {"outcome": "failed", "reason": "%s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)}
    try:
        return json.loads(proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip() or "{}")
    except ValueError:
        return {"outcome": "failed", "reason": "the adapter spoke something other than JSON"}


def resume(guard, cmd, handle, rec, model, bare):
    """Start the recorded session again, then read whether it is here.

    TWO FORMS, AND THE WRONG ONE MAKES A COPY. A session the harness has already
    run in the background carries its own saved options, and flags passed to a
    resume of it start a copy under a new session id. So a saved session is
    resumed bare and wakes with the name, model and permission flag it already
    has. A session with no saved options takes the flags, which is what sets
    those three the first time.

    The reading afterwards is the same one the twin check makes: a live process
    carrying this session id. The harness's own records answer it, so nothing
    here trusts the exit code of a command that returns before the session is up,
    and a resume that made a copy reads as a failure rather than a success.
    """
    session = rec["session"]
    argv = [cmd, "--bg", "--resume", session]
    if not bare:
        argv += ["--name", handle, "--model", model, "--dangerously-skip-permissions"]
    argv.append(BOOT_SENTENCE)
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            argv, cwd=rec["cwd"], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
            timeout=SPAWN_TIMEOUT_SECS)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        return False, "the harness did not return: %s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        tail = [l for l in proc.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").splitlines() if l.strip()]
        return False, "the harness exited %d: %s" % (
            proc.returncode, tail[-1].strip() if tail else "it wrote nothing to stderr")
    time.sleep(ALIVE_AFTER_SECS)
    if guard.generations(session):
        return True, "a process carries session %s" % session[:8]
    return False, "the harness exited 0 and no process carried the session %ds later" % ALIVE_AFTER_SECS


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the stop
def stop_pass(cmd, listing, idle_stop_minutes, act):
    """End the sessions this rail resumed that have gone quiet.

    A resumed session does not exit here. Measured 2026-09-25: one sat idle for 29
    minutes holding 351 MB and was still there. So the rail ends what it started,
    and an orchestrator's resting state stays what the design says it is, a
    registration and a workpaper with no process.

    ONLY WHAT THIS RAIL RESUMED, AND ONLY ONCE. The state file names the session
    each resume started, and a stop clears that name. Nothing else on the board is
    touched: a session somebody else started is somebody else's.

    QUIET MEANS TWO READINGS, NOT ONE. The harness's listing says idle, and the
    transcript has not grown for the threshold. The listing alone would end a
    session between two turns of work it is in the middle of.

    `claude stop` keeps the conversation, so the next pass resumes the same
    session from the same transcript. Nothing is destroyed.
    """
    out = []
    try:
        names = sorted(os.listdir(state_dir()))
    except OSError:
        return out
    for name in names:
        if not name.endswith(".json"):
            continue
        data = read_state(name[:-5])
        session = data.get("resumed_session")
        if not isinstance(session, str) or not session:
            continue
        handle = data.get("handle") or name[:-5]
        item = {"handle": handle, "session": session}
        row = (listing or {}).get(session)
        if listing is None:
            item["outcome"], item["detail"] = "waiting", "the harness's listing could not be read"
            out.append(item)
            continue
        if row is None or not row.get("pid"):
            item["outcome"] = "ended"
            item["detail"] = "it had already ended, so there was nothing to stop"
            if act:
                write_state(handle, {"resumed_session": None, "stopped_at": now_iso(),
                                     "stop_detail": item["detail"]})
            out.append(item)
            continue
        if row.get("status") != "idle":
            item["outcome"], item["detail"] = "working", "the listing reads %s" % row.get("status")
            out.append(item)
            continue
        path = transcript_path(session)
        quiet = None
        try:
            quiet = (time.time() - os.path.getmtime(path)) / 60.0 if path else None
        except OSError:
            quiet = None
        if quiet is None:
            item["outcome"] = "waiting"
            item["detail"] = "no transcript for it, so nothing says how long it has been quiet"
            out.append(item)
            continue
        item["quiet_minutes"] = round(quiet, 1)
        if quiet < idle_stop_minutes:
            item["outcome"] = "waiting"
            item["detail"] = ("quiet %.1f of the %d minutes" % (quiet, idle_stop_minutes))
            out.append(item)
            continue
        if not act:
            item["outcome"], item["detail"] = "would stop", "quiet %.1f minutes" % quiet
            out.append(item)
            continue
        # THE JOB ID, NOT THE SESSION ID. The listing carries both, and only the
        # short `id` addresses a job: `claude stop <session id>` answers "No job
        # matching". Measured 2026-09-25 against a session whose two fields
        # differ only in length, so a reader cannot tell them apart by eye.
        job = row.get("id") or session
        try:
            proc = subprocess.run([cmd, "stop", job], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                                  stderr=subprocess.PIPE, timeout=60)
            ok = proc.returncode == 0
            why = proc.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip().splitlines()
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
            ok, why = False, ["%s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)]
        item["job"] = job
        item["outcome"] = "stopped" if ok else "failed"
        item["detail"] = ("quiet %.1f minutes, the conversation is kept" % quiet if ok
                          else (why[-1] if why else "the harness gave no reason"))
        if ok:
            write_state(handle, {"resumed_session": None, "stopped_at": now_iso(),
                                 "stop_detail": item["detail"]})
        log("%s: stop %s -- %s" % (handle, item["outcome"], item["detail"]))
        out.append(item)
    return out


# ------------------------------------------------------------------- the pass
def survey(guard, listing, bus_dir, me, only=()):
    """One row per orchestrator handle this account holds, with what a pass owes it.

    Every such handle is listed, including the quiet ones, because `--check` is
    read to learn what the rail covers and a handle left out of it reads exactly
    like a handle the rail does not know about.

    `only` narrows what a pass acts on, and nothing else. The rows outside it are
    still listed and still carry the action they would have taken, so the reading
    is of the whole board whatever the pass touches.
    """
    rows = []
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(bus_dir, "handles.json"), "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            handles = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        return rows, "cannot read the board at %s: %s" % (bus_dir, exc)
    if not isinstance(handles, dict):
        return rows, "the board at %s is not a JSON object" % bus_dir

    live = guard.live_records()
    saved = saved_sessions(listing)
    for handle, rec in sorted(handles.items()):
        if not isinstance(rec, dict) or rec.get("role") != ROLE or rec.get("owner") != me:
            continue
        unread, newest = unread_count(bus_dir, handle)
        row = {"handle": handle, "session": rec.get("session") or "", "unread": unread,
               "newest_id": newest, "address": rec.get("address") or "",
               "cwd": rec.get("cwd") or ""}
        rows.append(row)
        if unread <= 0:
            row["action"], row["reason"] = "idle", "no mail, nothing to do"
            continue
        if not row["session"]:
            row["action"] = "refused"
            row["reason"] = "the record carries no session id, so there is nothing to resume"
            continue
        if not (os.path.isdir(row["cwd"]) and os.access(row["cwd"], os.X_OK)):
            row["action"] = "refused"
            row["reason"] = ("the recorded directory %s is not one this account can enter"
                             % (row["cwd"] or "<none>"))
            continue
        carriers = guard.generations(row["session"], live)
        wanted = {handle, row["address"], LISTING_REF.sub("", row["address"])} - {""}
        named = [r for r in live
                 if r["name"] in wanted and r["session"] != row["session"]]
        if carriers:
            top = carriers[-1]
            row["action"] = "nudge"
            row["pid"] = top["pid"]
            row["reason"] = ("session %s already runs in pid %d, so the session is here"
                             % (row["session"][:8], top["pid"]))
            continue
        if named:
            top = named[-1]
            row["action"] = "nudge"
            row["pid"] = top["pid"]
            row["reason"] = ("pid %d carries the name '%s' under session %s, so the "
                             "conversation moved and the session is here"
                             % (top["pid"], top["name"], top["session"][:8]))
            continue
        # WHICH RESUME FORM. A listing that could not be read decides nothing,
        # and a flagged resume of a saved session is the one outcome nothing
        # later can undo, so an unreadable listing is refused.
        if saved is None:
            row["action"] = "refused"
            row["reason"] = ("the harness's session listing could not be read, so nothing "
                             "says which resume form this session takes")
            continue
        row["bare"] = row["session"] in saved
        row["action"] = "resume"
        row["reason"] = "no process carries the session and no live session carries its name"
        if row["bare"]:
            row["model_source"] = "the session's saved options"
            continue
        model, path = transcript_model(row["session"])
        row["model"] = model or (conf_value("ORCHESTRATE_DELEGATE_MODEL") or "")
        row["model_source"] = "the transcript" if model else CONF
        row["transcript"] = path
        if not row["model"]:
            row["action"] = "refused"
            row["reason"] = ("no model: this session carries no saved options, the transcript "
                             "records none and %s names no ORCHESTRATE_DELEGATE_MODEL" % CONF)
    return rows, ""


def run_pass(guard, rail, adapter_dir, cmd, bus_dir, me, only=(), idle_stop_minutes=IDLE_STOP_MINUTES,
             act=True):
    listing = listed_sessions(cmd)
    rows, why = survey(guard, listing, bus_dir, me, only)
    result = {"bus_dir": bus_dir, "user": me, "only": sorted(only),
              "idle_stop_minutes": idle_stop_minutes,
              "held": [h for h, _ in holds()], "rows": rows,
              "stops": stop_pass(cmd, listing, idle_stop_minutes, act)}
    if why:
        result["blocking"] = [why]
        return result, 3
    if not act:
        return result, 0
    for row in rows:
        if row["action"] not in ("resume", "nudge"):
            continue
        handle = row["handle"]
        if only and handle not in only:
            row["outcome"] = "skipped"
            row["detail"] = "this pass was scoped to %s" % ", ".join(sorted(only))
            continue
        if row["action"] == "nudge":
            got = nudge(rail, adapter_dir, bus_dir, handle, {"owner": me,
                        "address": row["address"], "cwd": row["cwd"]}, row["pid"])
            row["outcome"] = got.get("outcome", "failed")
            row["detail"] = got.get("reason") or got.get("detail") or ""
            write_state(handle, {"last_wake": now_iso(), "last_wake_outcome": row["outcome"],
                                 "last_wake_detail": row["detail"]})
            log("%s: nudged pid %d -- %s %s" % (handle, row["pid"], row["outcome"], row["detail"]))
            continue
        if result["held"]:
            row["action"], row["outcome"] = "held", "skipped"
            row["detail"] = ("this account is held by %s, and no handle is resumed until "
                             "--clear-hold clears it" % ", ".join(result["held"]))
            log("%s: %s" % (handle, row["detail"]))
            continue
        ok, detail = resume(guard, cmd, handle, row, row.get("model") or "", row["bare"])
        row["outcome"] = "resumed" if ok else "failed"
        row["detail"] = detail
        patch = {"last_resume": now_iso(), "last_resume_outcome": row["outcome"],
                 "last_resume_detail": detail, "last_resume_model": row.get("model") or "",
                 "last_resume_bare": row["bare"]}
        if ok:
            patch["hold"] = None
            # What this rail started is what this rail ends. The stop pass reads
            # this name, and a stop clears it.
            patch["resumed_session"] = row["session"]
            patch["stopped_at"] = None
        else:
            patch["hold"] = {"at": now_iso(), "reason": detail, "model": row["model"]}
            patch["hold_told_human"] = tell_human(bus_dir, handle, detail)
            result["held"].append(handle)
        write_state(handle, patch)
        log("%s: resume %s -- %s" % (handle, row["outcome"], detail))
    return result, 0


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the modes
def cmd_law():
    fields = sorted(law_fields())
    percent = PERCENT_FIELD.search(BOOT_SENTENCE)
    print(json.dumps({"sentence": BOOT_SENTENCE, "format_fields": fields,
                      "percent_fields": percent.group(0) if percent else None,
                      "lawful": lawful()}, indent=2))
    if not lawful():
        log("THE SENTENCE LAW IS BROKEN: the boot sentence interpolates %s, and the ruling "
            "allows nothing at all." % (fields or [percent.group(0)]))
        return 1
    return 0


def cmd_clear_hold(handle):
    data = read_state(handle)
    if not data:
        log("no state for '%s' at %s" % (handle, state_path(handle)))
        return 0
    had = data.get("hold")
    write_state(handle, {"hold": None, "hold_cleared_at": now_iso()})
    print(json.dumps({"handle": handle, "cleared": bool(had),
                      "was": had, "state": state_path(handle)}, indent=2))
    return 0


def main(argv):
    args = list(argv)
    if not args or args[0] in ("-h", "--help"):
        sys.stderr.write(__doc__)
        return 2
    mode = args[0]
    if mode == "--law":
        return cmd_law() if len(args) == 1 else 2
    if mode == "--clear-hold":
        return cmd_clear_hold(args[1]) if len(args) == 2 else 2
    idle_stop = IDLE_STOP_MINUTES
    if "--idle-stop-minutes" in args[1:]:
        i = args.index("--idle-stop-minutes")
        if len(args) <= i + 1 or not args[i + 1].isdigit():
            sys.stderr.write("--idle-stop-minutes takes a whole number of minutes\n")
            return 2
        idle_stop = int(args[i + 1])
        del args[i:i + 2]
    only = set(args[1:])
    if mode not in ("--check", "--once") or (mode == "--check" and only) \
            or any(a.startswith("-") for a in only):
        sys.stderr.write("usage: session-continuity [--check | --once [<handle> ...]] "
                         "[--idle-stop-minutes <n>] | --law | --clear-hold <handle>\n")
        return 2

    # The law is proved before anything is delivered, every run. A build that
    # broke it must not reach a session at all.
    if not lawful():
        log("refusing to run: the boot sentence interpolates. See --law.")
        return 1

    me = pwd.getpwuid(os.geteuid()).pw_name
    bus_dir = conf_value("ORCHESTRATE_SHARED_BUS") or ""
    guard, guard_why = load_module(beside("session-guard"), "session-guard")
    rail, rail_why = load_module(beside("bus-nudge"), "bus-nudge")
    adapter_dir = beside("bus-nudge-adapters")
    cmd = harness()

    # EVERY WALL IS NAMED, AND A MISSING NUDGE IS ONE OF THEM. The twin check's
    # safe branch is a nudge. Without the rail beside it this program would find
    # a twin and have nowhere to send it, and the only move left would be the
    # resume the check just refused.
    blocking = []
    if not bus_dir:
        blocking.append("%s names no ORCHESTRATE_SHARED_BUS, so this machine declares no "
                        "bus and this program acts on none" % CONF)
    elif not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(bus_dir, "handles.json")):
        blocking.append("%s names %s as the bus and there is no board there"
                        % (CONF, bus_dir))
    if guard is None:
        blocking.append(guard_why + ". It says which processes carry a session, which is "
                        "half the twin check")
    if rail is None:
        blocking.append(rail_why + ". Its sentence is the one a twin is nudged with")
    elif not callable(getattr(rail, "nudge_text", None)):
        blocking.append("the wake rail at %s has no nudge_text()" % beside("bus-nudge"))
    if not os.access(os.path.join(adapter_dir, "claude"), os.X_OK):
        blocking.append("no wake-rail adapter at %s/claude, so a twin could not be nudged"
                        % adapter_dir)
    if not cmd:
        blocking.append("no 'claude' command for this account, so no session can be resumed")

    if mode == "--check":
        out = {"user": me, "conf": CONF, "bus_dir": bus_dir, "state_dir": state_dir(),
               "session_guard": beside("session-guard"), "wake_rail": beside("bus-nudge"),
               "adapter_dir": adapter_dir, "harness": cmd,
               "fallback_model": conf_value("ORCHESTRATE_DELEGATE_MODEL") or "",
               "sentence": BOOT_SENTENCE, "alive_after_secs": ALIVE_AFTER_SECS,
               "idle_stop_minutes": idle_stop,
               "holds": [{"handle": h, "hold": v} for h, v in holds()],
               "ready": not blocking, "blocking": blocking}
        if not blocking:
            checked, _ = run_pass(guard, rail, adapter_dir, cmd, bus_dir, me,
                                  idle_stop_minutes=idle_stop, act=False)
            out["rows"] = checked["rows"]
            out["stops"] = checked["stops"]
            if checked.get("blocking"):
                out["ready"], out["blocking"] = False, checked["blocking"]
        print(json.dumps(out, indent=2))
        return 0 if out["ready"] else 3

    if blocking:
        for line in blocking:
            log("refusing to run: %s" % line)
        return 3

    # BEFORE THE PASS READS THE STATE DIRECTORY, and only on a pass that acts.
    # --check returns above this line, so a reading changes nothing on disk.
    dropped = prune_state()
    if dropped:
        log("pruned state for %s: nothing has written to it in thirty days"
            % ", ".join(dropped))

    result, code = run_pass(guard, rail, adapter_dir, cmd, bus_dir, me, only, idle_stop)
    result["pruned"] = dropped
    acted = [r for r in result["rows"] if r.get("outcome") not in (None, "skipped")]
    # UNDER THE JOURNAL, ONE LINE A PASS, AND THE WHOLE RESULT ONLY WHEN IT ACTED.
    #
    # The sweeper beside this program writes nothing at all on a quiet pass,
    # because a result block every five minutes per account is the journal volume
    # the wake rail was fixed for. This rail starts sessions, and silence about
    # that is worth more than the bytes: a stopped instance and a quiet board
    # read the same, which is the failure wake-rail.md names about the rail
    # itself. So a pass says what it saw and what it decided in one short line,
    # and the block that names every handle is kept for a pass that acted.
    #
    # The cost is stated rather than assumed: one line every two minutes is about
    # seven hundred a day per account, against the forty-three thousand a day the
    # loop this rail sits beside was fixed for.
    if os.environ.get("JOURNAL_STREAM"):
        said = ["%s %s" % (r["handle"], r.get("outcome") or r["action"])
                for r in result["rows"] if r["action"] != "idle"]
        said += ["%s %s" % (t["handle"], t["outcome"]) for t in result["stops"]]
        log("%d orchestrator handle(s), %d with mail, %s" % (
            len(result["rows"]),
            len([r for r in result["rows"] if r["unread"] > 0]),
            ", ".join(said) or "nothing to do"))
        if acted or [t for t in result["stops"] if t["outcome"] in ("stopped", "failed")]:
            print(json.dumps(result))
    else:
        print(json.dumps(result, indent=2))
    return code


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
